  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain-free and mild conditions through the end of\nOctober\n\nLatest model guidance indicates the lengthy dry spell will last\nthrough Halloween and probably into the first few days of\nNovember. There appears to be a modest multi-model/ensemble \nsignal building toward the possibility of a frontal type heavy \nrain/wind event sometime during the second half of next week \naround the Nov. 5-7th timeframe. \n\nIn the meantime, early morning fog/low clouds give way to a very\nwarm afternoon Thursday with max temps challenging daily records\nin some locations (+10-20 degrees above the historical average).\n\nOctober 29th also marks two historic weather events to impact\ncentral Pennsylvania in the past 15 years: Sandy in 2012 and the\nSnowstorm in 2011. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Fire weather concerns increasing by Friday \n\nIt has been almost two weeks since we've received any measurable\nrainfall across Central PA. The combination of drying fuels,\nincreasing leaf litter, and low humidity air could result in an\nelevated risk of wildfire spread Friday and Saturday. Friday\nwill be breezy in the wake of a cold front with marginally low\nafternoon RH values and max gusts forecast in the 20-25 mph \nrange. PA Bureau of Forestry partners have indicated fuels are \nnow sufficiently dry to support the issuance of a wildfire \nspread statement /if needed/ for Friday.",
